# Auth for the Claude Code CLI inside the sandbox — one of:
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=   # from `claude setup-token`
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# GitHub token for `gh` inside the sandbox (issues read/comment/close).
# A fine-grained PAT with Issues read/write on design4pro/planned.travel is enough.
# GH_TOKEN=

# The shared run registry every sandcastle run claims its issues from, so two runs never build the
# same ticket - here, on another machine, or in GitHub Actions. Staging is the only coordinator
# (docs/adr/0009): the Durable Object is bound in `env.staging` alone, because a second registry
# would let two fleets each believe they hold the backlog.
#
# Both are required unless you pass --no-coordinator, which runs unco-ordinated and prints a loud
# banner saying so. Unset, a run stops at startup rather than starting blind: not knowing what
# another run holds is indistinguishable from there being no other run, and only one of those is safe.
#
# Read HOST-side, unlike everything else in this file - the coordinator client runs in main.ts, not
# in the sandbox - so the commented-out-key gotcha at the bottom does not apply to these two. A
# shell variable of the same name still wins over this file.
# SANDCASTLE_COORDINATOR_URL=https://staging.planned.travel
# SANDCASTLE_COORDINATOR_TOKEN=

# Cloudflare Access service token, because staging sits behind Access: without these the
# coordinator request is answered by a login page rather than by the Worker. Same pair the deploy
# workflows use for their smoke checks (CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID / CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET secrets).
# CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID=
# CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Only needed when a sandbox runs the Playwright suite (setup-e2e.sh) and the
# basemap is not already cached in .e2e-tiles/: R2:read on planned-travel-tiles.
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
# Which archive to PULL from the production bucket to seed this sandbox's local R2 simulator -
# dated and immutable (docs/DEPLOY.md 7.4), e.g. basemaps/planet-20260818.pmtiles. The same name CI
# reads as a repository variable and the cloud environment sets, because it is the same object.
#
# Not to be confused with TILES_ARCHIVE_KEY, which is the Worker var naming what /api/tiles/
# serves out of whichever bucket the Worker is bound to. setup-e2e.sh writes that one itself
# (basemaps/e2e.pmtiles, the key it loads the download into locally); this one is where the bytes
# come from.
# E2E_TILES_KEY=

# Sentry, for `pnpm sandcastle:sentry`. A User Auth Token (Settings -> Account -> API -> Auth
# Tokens), NOT an org token and NOT a DSN. The routine runs the MCP server with --skills=inspect,
# so it is read-only and needs exactly four scopes: org:read, project:read, team:read, event:read.
# Do not grant project:write / team:write / event:write - they buy nothing here and are standing
# risk. This is a different token from the SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN that uploads source maps in CI:
# different scopes, different blast radius, rotated independently.
#
# Gotcha worth the line: sandcastle's env resolver iterates the keys that appear in THIS file and
# takes `sandcastleEnv[key] || process.env[key]`. A commented-out line is not a key. Uncomment
# these even when the value is exported in your shell, or nothing reaches the sandbox.
# SENTRY_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# Both fall back to the conventional SENTRY_ORG / SENTRY_PROJECT that the source-map upload
# already reads, so configuring the build is usually enough. Defaults: design4pro / planned-travel.
# SENTRY_ORG_SLUG=
# Comma-separated if there is ever more than one project.
# SENTRY_PROJECT_SLUGS=

# Polar, for a ticket that touches billing. Optional: without these an agent gets everything
# except checkout, and `requirePrice()` refuses with a message naming the missing id.
#
# SANDBOX organization only. A production token here is a live payments credential inside a
# disposable container an agent can run anything in, and a production product id opens a checkout
# against a real account - neither fails loudly, which is what makes it worth stating rather than
# assuming. The sandbox and production organizations are separate accounts with separate ids
# (docs/DEPLOY.md §9.1).
#
# All seven or none. Three are the Worker's and four are the client bundle's, and half a set is a
# pricing page that offers one plan and breaks the other three with no error (§10).
#
# setup-e2e.sh writes the first three into the sandbox's throwaway .dev.vars and the last four into
# .env.local, because Vite reads one file and Miniflare the other.
# POLAR_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# POLAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# POLAR_SERVER=sandbox
# VITE_POLAR_PRODUCT_SOLO_MONTHLY=
# VITE_POLAR_PRODUCT_SOLO_YEARLY=
# VITE_POLAR_PRODUCT_CREW_MONTHLY=
# VITE_POLAR_PRODUCT_CREW_YEARLY=
